    "reasoning": "All 16 selected answers agree with independent mathematics and their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-4771: v(t)=2t-10 vanishes at t=5. q-48911: v(t)=(2t-4)e^{t²-4t+6}<0 exactly on [0,2). q-73210: v(t)=e^{-2t}(1-2t)>0 exactly on [0,1/2). q-7188: a(t)=-4t/3+6 vanishes at t=4.5, where v=27.5. q-109302: a(t)=12t-18 vanishes at t=3/2, where v=-21/2. q-49677: evaluating e^{2x}(x/2-1/4) between 0 and 1/2 gives 1/4. q-74087: differentiating x sin(2x+8)+cos(2x+8)/2 gives 2x cos(2x+8). q-88824: the denominator (2x+3)² is positive wherever defined, so positivity requires x>1. q-73286: [-cos x] from 0 to π/3 equals 1/2. q-103658: [-csc x] from π/4 to π/2 equals √2-1. q-141314: the positive ratio 11/9>1 implies divergence to positive infinity. q-141310: the ratio -2/3 has magnitude below 1, so the limit is 0. q-141323, q-141332, and q-74633: their positive ratios 8/x, 2/x², and 2/x must be at most 1, giving x≥8, x≥√2, and x≥2 respectively; equality gives a constant, convergent sequence. q-11311: the geometric area is the integral of y-y²/2 from 0 to 2, which equals 2/3. Its source incorrectly labels the negative signed integral as A in the intermediate calculation, but explicitly corrects the sign and states the correct final area. There is no final-answer disagreement. No submitted answer or grade was supplied; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions.",
